Hi Dave,

Thanks for the resend and for the extra OrangePi R2S testing report.
Went through both patches and have some questions/comments before this
is ready to merge.

Patch 1/2 ("do not require a PMIC or an SPI controller in SPL"):

> -=09if (ret)
> -=09=09panic("Fail to detect PMIC (%d)\n", ret);
> +=09if (ret) {
> +=09=09/*
> +=09=09 * Not every K1 board describes its PMIC to the SPL, and on some
> +=09=09 * the rails are already up when the SPL runs. Only boards whose
> +=09=09 * DDR bring-up depends on programming the bucks need one, so a
> +=09=09 * missing PMIC is not fatal here.
> +=09=09 */
> +=09=09log_info("No PMIC (%d), leaving rails as found\n", ret);
> +=09=09return;
> +=09}

- This turns "no PMIC node" and "PMIC node present but probe failed"
  into the same code path. uclass_get_device() returning -ENODEV is a
  legitimate "board has none" case, but -EIO/-ETIMEDOUT/etc. from an
  I2C bus that *is* populated with a PMIC looks identical here. On
  BPI-F3/MusePi Pro, a flaky I2C bus or a bad regulator strap would
  now silently leave vdd_core/vdd_1v8/vdd_mmc unprogrammed and boot
  on undervolted/wrong rails instead of panicking — that's strictly
  worse than today's behavior for boards that *do* ship a PMIC. Worth
  checking `ret == -ENODEV` explicitly (or whatever uclass returns for
  "no such DT node") rather than treating every error the same way,
  so real probe failures on PMIC-equipped boards still panic.
- Same question applies to the SPI controller hunk below it.

> +=09ret =3D uclass_get_device(UCLASS_SPI, 0, &dev);
> -=09if (ret)
> -=09=09panic("Fail to detect spi controller.\n");
> +=09if (ret) {
> +=09=09log_info("No SPI controller (%d)\n", ret);
> +=09=09return;
> +=09}

- Is this change gated by any Kconfig (e.g. only compiled when a
  board that lacks SPI NOR is selected), or does it apply to every K1
  target unconditionally? If it's unconditional, please say so
  explicitly in the commit message — right now the commit log implies
  it's safe for "boards that boot from eMMC or microSD" but the code
  has no way to know which board it's running on at this point, so
  the reasoning needs to hold for every K1 board that includes this
  SPL, not just the ones you tested.

Patch 2/2 ("fall back to the other boot devices in SPL"):

> +=09if (CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(MMC)) {
> +=09=09fallbacks[m++] =3D BOOT_DEVICE_MMC1;
> +=09=09fallbacks[m++] =3D BOOT_DEVICE_MMC2;
> +=09}
> +=09if (CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(SPI_LOAD))
> +=09=09fallbacks[m++] =3D BOOT_DEVICE_SPI;
> +
> +=09spl_boot_list[n++] =3D spl_boot_device();
> +=09for (i =3D 0; i < m; i++)
> +=09=09if (fallbacks[i] !=3D spl_boot_list[0])
> +=09=09=09spl_boot_list[n++] =3D fallbacks[i];
> +=09spl_boot_list[n] =3D BOOT_DEVICE_NONE;

- fallbacks[] is fixed-size 3 (MMC1, MMC2, SPI) and m can reach 3,
  which matches, but this is fragile — if another CONFIG_IS_ENABLED()
  branch is added later without bumping the array size it's a stack
  overflow with no compiler warning. A comment pinning
  "ARRAY_SIZE(fallbacks) must cover every fallback source below" (or
  sizing with a constant) would help the next person who touches this.
- Fallback order is always MMC1, MMC2, then SPI regardless of which
  device spl_boot_device() actually detected. For the OrangePi R2S
  case you describe (BootROM tries SD/MMC2 first, then loads FSBL from
  eMMC/MMC1), if detection ever returns MMC2 first, the current code
  still probes MMC1 before SPI, which seems right. But it's worth
  confirming this ordering was intentional rather than coincidental,
  since the commit message talks about "the other MMC controller"
  (singular) while the code always appends *both* MMC1 and MMC2 minus
  whatever matched spl_boot_device() — please double check the case
  where spl_boot_device() itself already returns BOOT_DEVICE_SPI, to
  make sure MMC1/MMC2 are still tried as fallbacks and not just SPI
  alone.
- This changes boot-device search order for *every* K1 board, not
  just the PMIC/SPI-NOR-less ones from patch 1. On BPI-F3/MusePi Pro,
  if spl_boot_device() ever mis-detects (unlikely, but this series
  exists because BootROM state can be ambiguous), the SPL will now
  silently try loading U-Boot from an MMC controller or SPI NOR that
  it previously would never have touched. Is that acceptable, or
  should board_boot_order() be scoped (e.g. via a Kconfig symbol
  selected only by the boards that need it) rather than applying
  globally?
- Please also confirm spl_spi_load_image() cleanly reports "no
  image"/returns an error rather than hanging when BOOT_DEVICE_SPI is
  reached on a board with no SPI NOR populated (patch 1 already copes
  with "no SPI controller", but this is "controller present, no
  flash/valid image").

Process note: neither the original two patches (patchwork IDs 2315364
and 2315366) nor this RESEND carry any Reviewed-by/Acked-by tags, and
I don't see any changelog in the commit messages between the original
postings and the resend — the cover letter just says "No changes." Can
you confirm whether this RESEND is meant to supersede 2315364/2315366
outright, so the maintainers can mark those two as superseded in
patchwork rather than carrying duplicate entries?

Verdict: not ready to merge as-is. Please address the -ENODEV vs.
other-errno distinction in patch 1, clarify the Kconfig scoping for
both patches, and confirm the boot-order fallback behavior described
above, then resend as v2 (or reply here if I've misread the probe
semantics).

Thanks,
Leo
